Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 19:08:56 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Nutrition was: topics?

> From: cbmvax!snark.thyrsus.com!esr@uunet.uu.net (Eric S. Raymond)

> > I'm sorry, Eric, but this is silly and not borne out by the evidence.
> 
> What kind of evidence would you consider sufficient?  I've got lots.  And your
> focus on B12 is too narrow; there are other things very difficult to get
> from a vegan diet.  Zinc, for example.

Evidence, please. All that I have says that there are plenty of vegetable
sources of zinc.

> 
> > If you need more calories, you can just eat more. Most people's bodies are
> > structured to give them very clear signals that they aren't eating enough.
> 
> Think so?  In fact, many peoples' hunger/satiety signals are quite unreliable;
> that's why so many people have overating/undereating problems.

Undereating problems are extremely rare. They mostly stem from psychological
disorders, as in anorexia. I find it very hard to believe that someone who
realized that they weren't getting enough calories couldn't just eat more.
There are some scrawny folk out there, but they rarely starve to death
by accident -- and they are usually capable of eating more if they choose.

> 
> > > Human beings are not really designed to be vegetarians --- our dentition,
> > > digestion, and dietary requirements are those of omnivores.
> > 
> > Quite false, actually. True omnivores like Bears have much shorter digestive
> > tracts and teeth designed for eating meat. Humans CAN digest meat, but
> > we don't seem to be built for a true omnivorous diet. Our gut length, teeth
> > and other factors seem to mark us out as creatures primarily designed
> > for a vegetarian diet with the capacity to eat meat when it is available.
> 
> I stand by my previous statement.  Human dentition is particularly telling;
> it includes teeth specialized for tearing and macerating meat.

Oh really? Which ones? We don't have true canines. Have a look at a cat's
jaw, and a dog's jaw, and a bear's jaw, and compare it to a cow's jaw and
a human's jaw sometime. We don't have the sort of teeth you need for a real
meat eating diet. We CAN eat meat, but none of ours are really well suited
to it.

>This is not
> a trivial phenotypic investment.  Compare it with mammals of omnivorous habit,
> then with herbivores.  Just as telling is the fossil evidence from Man's
> ancestors; interestingly, most had *fewer* adaptations for meat-eating than we
> do. 

Evidence, please. So far as I know, we've got just about the same level
of adaptations for meat eating as the great apes -- few, but some.

> And, on top of all this, there's behavior.  Any theory that claims human
> beings are natural vegetarians has to deal with the fact that almost all humans
> on what you'd call a "natural" diet experience intense meat cravings --- in
> fact, there are hunter-gatherer cultures that have a separate basic word for
>  "meat hunger".

I've never had a meat craving in my life. I know lots of people who don't
get them. I've never even heard this argument before, and I've been in plenty
of debates with people claiming that humans are "natural omnivores". This
one is totally new to me. Got any studies to back you up on this?

Fact is, our ancestors seem to have eaten whatever they could get their hands
on, which was occassionally meat, but usually wasn't. We weren't built for
handling a regular supply of the stuff -- certainly not like we've got now.

>  
> >                 There are cultures around the world that live on nearly
> > vegan diets and are perfectly healthy.
> 
> I used to believe this myself, but am now convinced it's a myth (or, more
> charitably, that it depends on a loose reading of "nearly").  A vegetarian
> anthropologist friend of mine says the normal human pattern seems to include
> eating a lot of meat on "feast days".

I suggest you talk to a Jain sometime. Hell, even in our own land it was
unusual to eat meat on anything like a regular basis until the mid-19th
century unless you were rich. There are lots of vegans in the world, and
they aren't dead -- no matter what your anthropologist friend says. Heck,
Jains, strict Buddists, Rastas and Seventh Day Adventists live their whole
lives without a lick of animal products and are by all accounts healthier
than most of the population. (Actually, I might be wrong about the
Rastafarians; someone care to correct me on this?)

> > I'd say its the meat eaters that have to be careful -- and I'd also say
> > that the technological help required to manage having your coronary arteries
> > completely occluded by athrosclerotic plaques is far greater than that
> > required to survive on my diet.
> 
> Bzzt.  Recent studies show that in somewhere between 2/3 and 3/4 of the
> population, intake of animal fat has *absolutely no* effect on serum
> cholesterol and lipoprotein levels.  This hasn't made it to the diet books yet.

Well, then I guess that Dean Ornish's study was all junk then, and the
epidemiological studies comparing world populations were also all junk.
There have been repeated studies on this. Animal fat per se may be meaningless,
but meat is high in fat almost no matter what you do compared to, say,
rice or spinach. Cholesterol intake per se might be meaningless, but fat
intake and the amount of cholesterol you yourself produce are correlated.

Look, Eric, I have no trouble with you deciding to go out and eat a
steak at every meal. Its fine if you want to. Just don't go around telling
other people it's good for them, because it isn't.

> >> (Me?  Happy omnivore --- I live, effectively, on raw vegetables and (cooked)
> >> meat.  My own version of dietary virtue is avoiding heavily processed foods.
> >> I've tried cutting my meat intake as an experiment; it made me hungry all the
> >> time.
> >
> > Why didn't you just eat more?
> 
> I did.  It didn't work.  I still felt hungry all the time, and had spells
> of lightheadedness.  Often I had the weird experience that I felt hungry
> while my stomach was still distended from the last helping of veggies.

This sounds rather bizarre. I've never ever heard a complaint like this from
anyone I've ever met who went vegetarian. Are you certain it wasn't just
psychological? Or prehaps you tried to eat nothing but salads or something --
grains and legumes are much more filling. (Celery is, by the way, possibly
a negative calorie food, because it might actually take more energy to digest
it than it yields. Contrast that with, say, soybeans, which are fairly high
in fat.)

> It
> was extremely unpleasant, and turned me permanently skeptical about most of
> today's diet wisdom.

Well, all I know is this -- virtually everyone on my mom's side of the family
has died of heart disease and cancer in their 50s, other than my mom, who
went vegetarian a while back. I went vegetarian myself, completely
independant of this. I've never felt hungry, never had "meat cravings", never
wandered the streets at night looking for hamburgers. I have mostly not
taken any supplements at all -- only started that recently in response to
information on life extension. (Also got worried about the slight possibility
of a B12 deficiency since I'm such a fanatic vegetable washer, so I started
supplementing that). I've been healthy the whole time, and I've been at
this for years. I will NOT argue that my anecdotal evidence fits for everyone,
but there seem to be some statements you are making that seem like nonsense.
The notion that one couldn't just eat more if one was hungry, for instance,
seems somewhat odd -- especially given the large population of vegans in
the world. Ditto for the notion that you couldn't get all the minerals and
vitamins you need (other than, as has been noted, B12).

Over all, I'd say that the evidence is currently on my side in this argument.
Good solid scientific studies have been done, everything from controlled
experiments to population studies (which usually used immigrants as
controls). Maybe you don't like the idea of going vegan -- but its very
hard to argue, I'd say impossible actually, that it is in and of itself the
least bit harmful -- and it is increasingly hard to argue that a low fat
vegan diet is not far healthier than the normal american diet.

Perry


